401 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 289 0 R
/E (This report is based on desk review, legal analysis, and qualitative interviews with girls affected by school exclusion and child or early marriage. The desk review draws on international conventions and relevant legal instruments concerning education, child protection, discrimination, and child marriage, alongside current reporting from United Nations agencies and human rights organizations. Together, these sources provide both up-to-date data and legal and policy framing.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
402 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 289 0 R
/E (The interview component is qualitative and illustrative. It is not intended to establish national prevalence rates. Rather, it is used to illuminate how policy restrictions are experienced at the household and individual levels. Interview material has been anonymized and handled in accordance with safeguarding, confidentiality, informed consent, and referral protocols appropriate to work involving girls exposed to risk, coercion, or trauma.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
403 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 289 0 R
/E (The research team conducted in-depth interviews with 40 women drawn from 18 provinces of Afghanistan, capturing a range of regional, economic, and educational circumstances. All participants are identified in this report by pseudonyms; the names used throughout are not the real names of the women interviewed, and any potentially identifying details have been removed or altered to protect their safety. Interviews were conducted by voice, with consent, and transcribed and translated by the research team. Each transcript was then analysed using a thematic coding frame, in which segments of testimony were assigned to the six analytic themes that structure the Findings section: marriage risk, economic pressure, isolation and agency, protective pathways, physical and reproductive health, and psychological and emotional harm. Representative coded extracts are summarized and quoted within the Findings section, where each theme is developed alongside the testimony that supports it.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
